/-!
# Chern classes and sections

Let `(S, δ)` be the base situation of chapter 42 of the Stacks Project, let `X` be a scheme
locally of finite type over `S`, let `ℰ` be a finite locally free `𝒪_X`-module, let `f : X' ⟶ X`
be locally of finite type and let `s ∈ Γ(X', f^*ℰ)`. The *zero scheme* `Z(s) ⊆ X'` is the closed
subscheme whose quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals is the image of `s : (f^*ℰ)^∨ ⟶ 𝒪_{X'}`.

[Stacks, Tag 0FA9] says: if `dim_δ(X') = n`, if `f^*ℰ` has constant rank `r`, if
`dim_δ(Z(s)) ≤ n - r`, and if for every generic point `ξ ∈ Z(s)` with `δ(ξ) = n - r` the ideal
of `Z(s)` in `𝒪_{X', ξ}` is generated by a regular sequence of length `r`, then

  `c_r(ℰ) ∩ [X']_n = [Z(s)]_{n - r}`  in  `CH_*(X')`.

This file states that lemma and carries out the first step of its proof: because `c_r(ℰ)` is a
bivariant class for the identity of `X` ([Stacks, Tag 0B7H]), the general equality follows from
the equality on `X'` itself with the pulled-back module, which is the case `X = X'` the source
reduces to.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Chow.zeroSchemeOfSection` — the zero scheme of a global section of a module of any
  rank, the construction of the standing situation of [Stacks, Tag 0FA8].
* `Stacks.Chow.IsRegularZeroSchemeSequence` — the regular-sequence hypothesis of
  [Stacks, Tag 0FA9]: at every generic point of `Z(s)` of `δ`-dimension `m`, the ideal cut out
  by `Z(s)` in the local ring is generated by a regular sequence of length `r`.
* `Stacks.Chow.TopChernCapEqZeroScheme` — the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 0FA9] together with its
  hypotheses, in the reduced case `X = X'`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Chow.zeroSchemeOfSection_eq_zeroScheme` — for an invertible module the construction is
  the zero scheme of [Stacks, Tag 02OQ].
* `Stacks.Chow.cap_fundamentalCycle_eq_zeroSchemeCycle_of_isChernClassBivariant` —
  **[Stacks, Tag 0FA9], the statement in full together with the first step of its proof**: the
  equality for the bivariant class `c_r(ℰ) ∈ A^r(X)` capped against `[X']_n`, deduced from the
  reduced case `X = X'`.
* `Stacks.Chow.topChernCapEqZeroScheme_rank_one` — **the reduced case at `r = 1`**, the base case
  of the source's induction, discharged from [Stacks, Tag 02SQ].
* `Stacks.Chow.cap_fundamentalCycle_eq_zeroSchemeCycle_rank_one` —
  **[Stacks, Tag 0FA9] for a module of rank one, with no reduced case assumed**: the first result
  above with its hypothesis `hreduced` supplied by the second.

## Implementation notes

### Which cases of the induction on `r` are covered, and what the open ones need

The reduced case `X = X'` of the tag is proved by induction on the rank `r`. Of that
induction, **only `r = 1` is covered here** (`topChernCapEqZeroScheme_rank_one`, from Stacks
02SQ). Both remaining cases are open invitations, not closed doors:

* **`r = 0` is not covered.** Two inputs are missing. First, that a module of rank zero has
  vanishing sections — there is no rank-zero lemma anywhere under `StacksFrontier/Algebra/` — which
  is what makes the spanning set of `zeroSchemeOfSection` the zero ideal. Second, the
  identification of the fundamental cycle of the bottom ideal sheaf's closed immersion with that
  of the identity; nothing in the library relates `cycleAssociatedToClosedSubscheme` to `⊥`. The
  rank-zero sections lemma is the genuinely useful piece and looks cheap with the sheaf API in
  hand.
* **`r > 1` is not covered.** It needs the projective space bundle formula of Stacks 02TX — the
  decomposition of `CH_*(P)` making flat pullback along the projection injective. Only the
  flatness and relative-dimension clauses of 02TX are carried in this repository
  (`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveBundle.FlatAndRelativeDimension`), and the rest of
  this directory carries the formula as a hypothesis wherever it is needed.

### The cap product is a parameter, as everywhere in this directory

The cap product with the higher Chern classes is not constructed in this repository: Stacks 0B7G
is unlanded, and `Stacks.Chow.HigherChernCapRule` is the parameter standing in for it, exactly as
in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ChernClassHigher.Basic`,
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ChernClassHigher.BivariantClass` and
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.TopChernClass`. The bivariant class of Stacks 0B7H is built
from that parameter, so the statement below carries it too.

### What the first step of the proof is, in this encoding

A `Stacks.Chow.HigherChernCapRule` is already an operation on the Chow groups of *every* test
scheme, formed there with the pulled-back module; so the reduced case `X = X'` of the lemma is
the statement about `cap (f ≫ sX) (f^*ℰ)`, and the general case is the statement about the cap
operation of a bivariant class for `𝟙 X`. `Stacks.Chow.IsChernClassBivariant` is exactly the
identification of the second with the first, so the reduction the source performs with Stacks
0B7H is the one line below. The class itself is available: `Stacks.Chow.chernClassBivariant`
satisfies `Stacks.Chow.IsChernClassBivariant` by
`Stacks.Chow.isChernClassBivariant_chernClassBivariant`.

### What is not proved here, and why

The source proves the reduced case by induction on `r`. The case `r = 1` is Stacks 02SQ, proved
as `Stacks.ChernCap42.firstChernClassCapOfInvertible_closedSubscheme_eq_zeroScheme`; the step
`r > 1` runs over the projective space bundle `π : P ⟶ X` of `ℰ` and needs the *projective space
bundle formula*, Stacks 02TX, to know that it suffices to prove the equality after pulling back
along `π`. Only the flatness and relative-dimension clauses of Stacks 02TX are carried in this
repository (in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveBundle.FlatAndRelativeDimension`); the
decomposition of `CH_*(P)` that makes `π^*` injective is not, and the rest of the directory
carries it as a hypothesis wherever it is needed. So the reduced case is a hypothesis here,
named and docstringed, rather than a theorem.

### The rank hypothesis

The source assumes `ℰ` finite locally free and `f^*ℰ` of constant rank `r`. Forming `c_r(ℰ)` at
all requires the rank to be constant on `X` in this encoding — a module of non-constant rank is
handled by the rank decomposition, as in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.TopChernClass` —
so constant rank `r` is assumed on `X`, which gives it on `X'` by
`AlgebraicGeometry.isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_pullback`.

### The ideal cut out in a local ring

"The ideal of `Z(s)` in `𝒪_{X', ξ}`" is the kernel of the stalk map of the closed immersion
`Z(s) ⟶ X'` at the point of `Z(s)` over `ξ`, which is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.stalkIdeal` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.RelativeRegularImmersion.StalkIdeal` written out.
It is written out rather than imported so that this file depends only on the Chow directory.

